{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 0,
    "dDurationMs": 2400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Rocket Money for supporting PBS."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2400,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Two protons next to each other in an \natomic nucleus are repelling each other  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 6780,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electromagnetically with enough force to lift \na medium-sized labradoodle off the ground.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 12900,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Release this energy and you have, \nwell, you have a nuclear explosion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 17640,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just as well there's an even stronger force than \nthe electromagnetism holding our nuclei together.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 23820,
    "dDurationMs": 3480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it's not the strong force, as \nyou might have imagined. At least  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27300,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "not directly. Nuclei are held \ntogether by a quirk of nature,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 32520,
    "dDurationMs": 6540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "without which we would have no complex atoms, \nno chemistry, and certainly no labradoodles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 44880,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Quantum chromodynamics is a complicated \nsounding name, but the fundamental force  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 50460,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of nature it describes - the strong force - is \nactually even more complicated than it. sounds.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 55920,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In our previous episode on this topic we saw how \nthe strong force binds the elementary quarks into  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 62700,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "protons and neutrons. But that’s where we left \nit. Based on what we learned in that episode,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67980,
    "dDurationMs": 7620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the strong force is entirely confined to the \nnucleons, and yet somehow it’s also the force that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75600,
    "dDurationMs": 7140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "binds protons and neutrons together to form most \natomic nuclei. That process is strange enough that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82740,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it deserves its own episode - especially because \nit was this very question - how are atomic nuclei  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 89040,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "held together, that led to the discovery of both \nthe strong and weak nuclear forces in the first  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 94500,
    "dDurationMs": 9120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "place. But this is also the story of the meson - \nthe obscure cousin to the proton and neutron that,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103620,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "while less well known, is just as essential to the \nexistence of all complex matter in the universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 109680,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our story starts with Hideki Yukawa, a young \nphysicist who graduated from the University  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 116040,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of Osaka in 1929, before moving back in with his \nparents and working for no money as a university  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 122340,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "assistant. Due to the Great Depression, not \nbecause he wasn’t a great physicist. All the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128220,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "while, Yukawa was focused on the problem \nof how nucleons stuck together. He was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 133680,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perplexed about why a force so strong \nshouldn’t be observable outside atomic nuclei."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139860,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yukawa had one intriguing clue \nto the inner lives of nuclei,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143820,
    "dDurationMs": 6600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and that was beta decay - the tendency of large \nnuclei to occasionally spit out an electron,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 150420,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and in the process convert one \nof their neutrons into a proton."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155340,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He knew that the electromagnetic force is \ncommunicated by the exchange of photons,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160140,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and it stood to reason that this nuclear \nforce should be mediated by some particle.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167040,
    "dDurationMs": 1920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what if this particle was the electron,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168960,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and beta decay was the side effect of the \nexchange of electrons between nucleons?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 174600,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like, if protons and neutrons were playing catch \nwith the electron and sometimes they fumble the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 180720,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "ball. It turns out this is totally not what’s \nhappening, but the insight that forces should be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187080,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mediated by particles was new in its time, and \nit eventually led Yukawa to the right answer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 193140,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Having an exchange particle with \nmass, unlike the massless photon,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197280,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is a great first step for \nmaking a short-range force.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 202080,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These exchange particles are what we call \nvirtual particles - which we’ve discussed  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 206940,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "before. Virtual particles can sort of break the \nlaws of physics, in the sense that they can pluck  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 212880,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the energy needed for their existence out of \nnowhere, as long as they give it back again."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218820,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is a result of the Heisenberg uncertainty \nprinciple, which in one form says that we can  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 223920,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "never know both the energy and the duration of a \nphenomenon to better than a certain tiny accuracy.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 230700,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That means, for very short periods of time, the \namount of energy in a patch of space can vary."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 236340,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Mass is a form of energy, so a \nvirtual particle can briefly gain  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 241440,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass from nothing - but the more mass \nit has, the less time it can exist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245940,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yukawa used the electron mass to calculate the \nstrength and range of a force mediated by this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252300,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particle, assuming it’s traveling near the speed \nof light. The resulting force turned out to be  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 257160,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "around 1/200th of the strength needed to hold the \nnucleus together, and had a range much larger than  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 264180,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the size of the atomic nucleus. So the electron \ncouldn't really be the particle mediating this  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 269640,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nuclear force. But Yukawa now had an equation \nthat described the type of force he wanted."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 277260,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He adjusted the equation to describe a force \nwith the appropriate strength and range,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 282960,
    "dDurationMs": 3780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and found that it required a much \nmore massive exchange particle.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287280,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He called this hypothetical particle the \"meson\" \nfrom the Greek “mesos” for middle - because it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 293460,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "had to have a mass somewhere between \nthat of the proton and the electron."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 297360,
    "dDurationMs": 6420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yukawa also predicted that these particles had to \nhave electric charge. Just like with Beta Decay,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 303780,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a neutron could emit a negative \nmeson and transform into a proton,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309540,
    "dDurationMs": 3840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a proton could emit a positive \nmeson and transform into a neutron,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313380,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the neutral meson could be exchanged \nbetween protons or between neutrons.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 319380,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As a result, Yukawa predicted 3 mesons \ntypes, one for each possible charge value."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 325680,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Unfortunately this new theory didn’t explain \nbeta decay at all. In fact, Yukawa reasoned  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 331920,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that beta decay required a completely different \nnuclear force, much weaker than the one holding  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 337380,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the nucleus together. In fact, he correctly \npredicted the existence of both the strong and  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 342900,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the weak force at the same time. Although the \nweak force isn’t really mediated by electrons,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 348120,
    "dDurationMs": 4140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and we have videos explaining everything \nyou probably want to know about it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 352260,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yukawa published his theory in 1935, and it \nwas almost completely ignored. To be fair,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358920,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "proposing the existence of two new forces of \nnature was an extraordinary claim, and such claims  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 364980,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "require extraordinary evidence. No one was going \nto believe this until we’d detected a meson."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 371340,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These days we routinely create countless \nmesons by smashing atoms together in particle  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 377220,
    "dDurationMs": 3900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "accelerators. But back then particle \naccelerators were just being developed,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 381120,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so we had to rely on cosmic rays. These are\nhigh energy particles that rain down on  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 387120,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the Earth from natural space particle \naccelerators like supernovae and quasars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 393180,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Yukawa wrote a letter to Nature magazine \nexplaining why cosmic radiation could contain  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 399240,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mesons, hoping to inspire other researchers \nto look for these, but his letter it was rejected"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406020,
    "dDurationMs": 4380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nonetheless, others had independently thought \nof searching for new particles in cosmic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410400,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "radiation. We have Bibha Chowdhuri \nand Debendra Mohan Bose for example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 415680,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "who developed a method using photographic \nplates. They set these up at high altitudes  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 421200,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in India and Tibet due to the fact that our \natmosphere is so good at blocking cosmic  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 425880,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rays - which is annoying if you’re a particle \nphysicist, but great if you’re anyone else.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 431520,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And Chowdhuri and Bose did indeed spot the meson \nat around the mass Yukawa predicted. Sadly their  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437880,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "research went mostly unnoticed until British \nscientists independently found mesons in 1947."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 443700,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Anyway, not only were these new particles in the \nright mass range, there were three of them as  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 449400,
    "dDurationMs": 7620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "predicted by Yukawa, one positive, one negative, \nand one neutral. But then things got awkward."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 457020,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Over the next couple of years, thanks to better \nparticle accelerators and improved understanding  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 461820,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of radiation, more and more mesons were \ndiscovered, with a range of properties."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 466440,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The first mesons, the ones found by \nBose and Chowduri, are called pions,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 471780,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and these are the particles actually \nexchanged by protons and neutrons in  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 476820,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the nucleus. But there were many others like \nkaons, eta mesons, D mesons, B mesons, etc."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 483600,
    "dDurationMs": 4680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And to make matters worse, scientists also \nfound more particles that could \"exchange  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488280,
    "dDurationMs": 4920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mesons\" just like protons and neutrons. \nThese particles were named baryons,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 493200,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "after the Greek word for \"heavy\". \nThere was the Lambda, Sigma, Xi,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 498660,
    "dDurationMs": 4800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Omega, and more. Again, all with \ndifferent masses, charges and spins."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 503460,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This endless collection of baryons and mesons \ncame to be known as the Particle Zoo and it  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509760,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "was a huge problem for physics. According \nto Yukawa's theory, the atomic nucleus only  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 515220,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "needs two baryons - the proton and neutron \n- and three mesons—the pions. So what was  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 521100,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with all this extra junk that nature doesn’t \nseem to use outside of particle accelerators?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 526560,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This problem was finally solved \nby Murray Gell-Mann, who realized  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 530760,
    "dDurationMs": 6060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that mesons and baryons are not elementary \nparticles, but rather are made of quarks.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 536820,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hadron is the general name for a particle made \nof multiple quarks. The particle zoo just results  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 542760,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "from all the quark combinations that are possible. \nWe covered this in our episode on quantum  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548040,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "chromodynamics, but here’s a quick refresher \nso we can get to the punchline of this video."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 554040,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With the discovery of quarks, it was clear that \nwe needed the strong force to not only hold the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 559440,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nucleus together, but also hold the nucleons \ntogether. In fact, the latter is what the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 565020,
    "dDurationMs": 10440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "strong force really. Binding nucleons is sort of \nan afterthought. So just as electromagnetism works  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 575460,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on things with electric charge, the strong force \nworks on things with color charge. There are 3  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 581700,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "types of color charge, unlike the single type \nof electric charge. A quark, for example,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 586320,
    "dDurationMs": 6360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could have a colour charge of red, green, or \nblue, or the opposite colour charge of antired,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 592680,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "antigreen, or antiblue. These names by \nthe way are just analogies of course."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 596760,
    "dDurationMs": 6300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Composite particles need to be colour neutral, \nso the 3-quark baryons have one of each colour  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 603060,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "charge which cancel each other out, while 2 quarks \nof a meson will have a colour and its anti-colour."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 609780,
    "dDurationMs": 3780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These quarks are bound by the strong \nforce, which is mediated by a massless  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 613560,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particle called the gluon. Gluons each carry \none regular and one anti-colour charge,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 619680,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and they cause the colours of \nquarks to flip when absorbed.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 624300,
    "dDurationMs": 5100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Baryons and mesons are held together by \na continuous exchange of virtual gluons."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 629400,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But wait a minute, haven't we been saying \nthe entire time that the reason the Strong  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 634920,
    "dDurationMs": 4260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Force has a short range is because \nit is transmitted mesons with mass?  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 639780,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well that was Yukawa's whole deal, but now  \nit turns out mesons aren't the real exchange  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 645420,
    "dDurationMs": 5340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "particles of the strong force? And the real ones \n- the gluons - don't have mass? What's going on?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 650760,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Actually, the colour-neutral hadrons \ncan’t even feel the strong force directly,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654960,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just as electrically neutral objects don’t \nexert an electrostatic force on each other.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 660780,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there is a workaround. Atoms seem \nelectrically neutral from afar, but if  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 667440,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you are close enough to the nucleus you would feel \nits positive electric charge. Similarly if protons  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 674100,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or neutrons get close enough to each other, their \ninternal quarks will feel each other’s presence.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 679440,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A quark from, say, a proton, will want to \ntug at a quark from a neighboring neutron.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684840,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But they can’t simply exchange a gluon to \ndo that - because gluons have colour charge,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 689340,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "so exchanging a gluon would cause the hadrons to \nno longer be colour neutral, which is not allowed.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 695940,
    "dDurationMs": 3960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In order to properly feel the strong \nforce the hadrons need to exchange a  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 699900,
    "dDurationMs": 3120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "neutral particle. And nature has \nfound a way to make that happen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 703800,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Here’s how it goes. A pair of nucleons get close \nand a quark from one gets pulled towards the  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 710040,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "other. Its gluon connection to the other quarks in \nits nucleus - what we call a flux tube - extends  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 716700,
    "dDurationMs": 6960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and snaps, and in the process generates \ntwo new quarks. One remains in the nucleus,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 723660,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "while the other forms a quark-antiquark pair \nwith the original escaped quark. This is our  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 729780,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "meson - our pion. It’s colour neutral and so it \ncan be absorbed by the second nucleus without  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 735600,
    "dDurationMs": 6780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "breaking any physics. There, one of the meson’s \nquarks annihilates with an antiquark counterpart  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 742380,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in that nucleus. Ultimately we’re left with two \nnucleons of the same type that we started with,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 747240,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but they've now communicated the strong \nforce. They did it a roundabout way,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752520,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but the process nonetheless exchanged energy \nand momentum, binding the particles together."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 759180,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is how the strong force finds its \nway around its lack of a neutral gluon.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 763920,
    "dDurationMs": 4080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It cobbles together a neutral \nexchange particle - the meson."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 768000,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the meson has mass. A virtual meson has \nto borrow a lot of energy to create its mass,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 773940,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so the uncertainty principle says \nthat it can’t exist for very long. Its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 779460,
    "dDurationMs": 6840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "range defines the possible size of an atomic \nnucleus. If a nucleus gets too big then its  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 786300,
    "dDurationMs": 5400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "nucleons are too far apart to exchange mesons \neffectively, and it’ll decay. By the way,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 791700,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "when we talk about the force between \nquarks we call it the strong force,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 795900,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but this residual strong force that exists between \nnucleons is called the strong nuclear force."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802020,
    "dDurationMs": 5160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Ya know, this reminds me of our episode \nabout quasi particles. In that episode  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 807180,
    "dDurationMs": 4740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we talked about how fields can arise \nas emergent behaviors of other fields,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 811920,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and these quasifields will have their own \nquasiforces and quasiparticles. Well that's sort  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 817920,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of what's happening here. The strong nuclear force \nis a quasi-force mediated by a quasi-particle,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 823740,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the meson. Without this little quirk of nature \n- this emergence of a force that’s not really  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 829740,
    "dDurationMs": 4620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "supposed to be there, the most complex \natom in the universe would be hydrogen.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 834900,
    "dDurationMs": 6660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Fortunately nature stumbled on a way to bind the \nnuclei, which gave us chemistry, and biology,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 841560,
    "dDurationMs": 6120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and ourselves, including a young physicist \nnamed Hideki Yukawa, who was able to figure  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 847680,
    "dDurationMs": 7140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "out some of the most important forces from \nwhich emerges this richly complex spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 854820,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you to Rocket Money for supporting PBS. \nBecause life only gets more complicated, busy,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 860760,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and expensive, Rocket Money knows that managing \nyour money the easy way has never been more  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 866280,
    "dDurationMs": 6720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "critical. The Rocket Money finance app helps you \ncancel unwanted subscriptions, lower your bills,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873000,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "create custom budgets, and see where your money \nis going - all in one place. Rocket Money will  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 878880,
    "dDurationMs": 6000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "identify recurring charges for you, and you can \ncancel them right from the app with just a tap.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 884880,
    "dDurationMs": 5880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That way, you never have to make a call to a \nbiller or hunt down the \"cancel\" buttons one at  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 890760,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a time on each billers' website. Rocket Money does \nall the hard work for you. Rocket Money can also  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 896940,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "negotiate your bills for you, whether it’s for \ninternet, phone, or cable. Simply upload a photo,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 903180,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and Rocket Money takes care of the rest. Finally, \nyou can use Rocket Money to set budgets that  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 908460,
    "dDurationMs": 5220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "automatically monitor your spending by category. \nThe app will send you friendly notifications when  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 913680,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "you've exceeded your spending limit, and you \ncan track how much you’re saving over time.  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 917880,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Rocket Money is here to help members improve their \nfinancial habits - and they're here to help you,  "
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 923460,
    "dDurationMs": 5640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "too. For more information, click the link in \nthe description or scan the QR code on screen."
    } ]
  } ]
}
